WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.002 --> 00:00:12.991
Mission Control · The decision layer for IT product ownership in engineering · Your enterprise already has the systems of record. · This is what sits on top of them.

3
00:00:15.152 --> 00:00:24.501
Day 30: eight leadership interviews, synthesized. Code ranks the problems; every point traces to what people said.

4
00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:32.622
It also shows where leaders disagree, so I resolve that before building.

5
00:00:32.622 --> 00:00:41.577
The top complaint: engineering change is slow. Switch apps, same suite.

7
00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:56.147
PLM Insight measures it: median 18.7 days, and 3 in 10 ECOs are sent back.

8
00:00:56.147 --> 00:01:06.027
The AI reads 148 return comments and finds the causes. Code checks every cited ECO and quote, and does the counting.

9
00:01:06.027 --> 00:01:13.873
The fix is a control in the process, not a reminder email. One click sends it to the backlog.

11
00:01:19.922 --> 00:01:29.125
Rules find duplicate parts. The AI caught what my rules missed: these two have different export classifications.

12
00:01:29.125 --> 00:01:35.398
So merging them is now Trade Compliance's call, not the data steward's.

14
00:01:37.075 --> 00:01:46.555
Support Pilot groups 1,900 tickets into 14 recurring problems, without AI, and shows which have no article.

16
00:01:48.187 --> 00:01:59.462
For a gap, the AI drafts the article from how agents actually resolved it, and lists what it doesn't know.

18
00:02:07.975 --> 00:02:16.997
Asked something no article covers, it says so and writes the ticket, instead of guessing.

20
00:02:18.646 --> 00:02:29.349
App Portfolio covers what we pay for. The AI reads each contract; code checks every quoted term, then works out the notice deadlines.

21
00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:35.837
One renewal's notice window already closed; three more are due this month.

23
00:02:37.519 --> 00:02:47.008
PartFinder: the PLM platform already does all of it, and 118 of 880 licensed people use it. Rules propose Eliminate.

24
00:02:47.008 --> 00:02:52.282
Its licensed users come from Active Directory, read-only.

25
00:02:52.282 --> 00:03:00.413
I decide; overriding the rules would need a reason. The retirement work goes to the same backlog.

27
00:03:10.101 --> 00:03:20.806
Everything lands in one backlog: from interviews, ECO data, the help desk, and the portfolio, each with its evidence and a link back.

29
00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:30.576
Status is computed by rules, and early warnings show the numbers behind them.

31
00:03:32.227 --> 00:03:42.787
The weekly update says only what the data says: every number is checked against its source, and nothing is sent until I approve it.

32
00:03:42.787 --> 00:03:52.559
Every screen explains itself, and the rules reference is generated from the code that runs the rules, so it can't drift.

35
00:04:07.001 --> 00:04:39.208
What does Mission Control do that I can't already do with existing tools? · Connects the dots. One problem, from what people said to a cited update. · Shows its work. Code checks every AI claim before it is used. · Keeps people deciding. Overrides need a reason; every decision is recorded. · Explains itself. Built-in help, documented from the code. · Starts small. Reads exports and APIs; writes nothing to systems of record.

36
00:04:39.208 --> 00:04:50.053
Mission Control · Backlog Pilot · Leadership Pilot · PLM Insight · Support Pilot · App Portfolio · Built by LaVon Rutledge · Aenea Labs
